sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch05-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In multichambered organisms such as the foraminifera Globigerina or Rotalia, and in desmids, each newly formed bud or chamber is a figure of equilibrium shaped by surface tension while still fluid, and is then solidified onto earlier chambers, so the whole organism is not itself a figure of minimal area.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Each new foraminiferal chamber is a surface-tension figure; the whole shell is not";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter V, “The Forms of Cells”; topic “Forms fixed by successive solidification”. Scope: multichambered foraminifera, desmids and other organisms formed by successive stages of growth and consolidation"
}
